<header class="sp-head"><p class="featured-region">Winter 431–430 BCE · Athens</p><h1 class="sp-title">Pericles' Funeral Oration</h1><p class="sp-by"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pericles">Pericles</a></p></header>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>The setting</h2><p>In the first winter of the Peloponnesian War, Athens held a public funeral for its dead, and Pericles, the city's foremost statesman, was chosen to speak. Thucydides recorded the speech in his history, shaping it into a portrait of what Athenians believed their city to be.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>Key passage</h2><blockquote class="sp-quote"><p>Our constitution does not copy the laws of neighbouring states; we are rather a pattern to others than imitators ourselves.</p></blockquote><p class="sp-source">Thucydides, History of the Peloponnesian War, II.37, tr. Richard Crawley (1874).</p><div class="sp-full"><a class="sp-full-link" href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/History_of_the_Peloponnesian_War/Book_2#Pericles_Funeral_Oration" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the complete text<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a><span class="sp-full-src">Wikisource, tr. Richard Crawley</span></div></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>What followed</h2><p>Within two years plague had swept Athens and killed Pericles himself, and the long war ended in Athenian defeat in 404 BCE. Yet the speech outlived the city's empire, becoming a founding text of democratic thought and a model for commemorative oratory ever since.</p><div class="sp-led"><h3 class="sp-led-k">What it led to</h3><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plague-of-athens">Plague of Athens</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-aegospotami">Battle of Aegospotami</a></div></div></section>
